#show: chapter.with(
  title: [A Few Facts About Argentina],
)

Humans have existed in Argentina for a long time. The oldest that I know of were called Yahgans and the Onas and they lived in Tierra del Fuego. Tierra del Fuego is the southernmost province of Argentina. Yahgans and Onas weren't the words they used to call themselves by the way. It's just something the European colonizers chose to call them. Their actual names were quite different. The Onas would call themselves Selk'nam, and the Yahgans would call themselves Yamanas. Fine, Yamanas is sort of similar to Yahgan, but Selk'nam? I have no idea why Onas caught on.

Horses have lived in Argentina for a lot longer. In fact, the earliest horse species lived in the Americas about 50 million years ago. During an ice age, when the sea level was low enough that Alaska and Siberia were connected by land, the horses spread to Eurasia, and later they died out in the Americas. Much later, when the Europeans "discovered" the Americas again, they brought with them some of their Eurasian horses, not knowing that what they were bringing were native to that land.

Yahgans and Onas were alive before the monotheistic religions were invented in Eurasia and yet they had an advanced moral system of their own, which was more similar than different to the western moral system we are now familiar with. Both tribes had a sense of right and wrong and they wouldn't harm others unless they thought there was a good reason to. 

Yahgans were very anti-murder but Onas had many legal reasons for murdering someone. If you wanted a wife but she was already married, you could murder her husband and take her home with you. If someone murdered your friend, you could murder them in revenge, and also preemptively murder all their friends and family so they wouldn't murder you back in revenge. If you were the doctor of a village and someone in the neighbouring village died of a disease, the villagers could murder you with the rationale that you must have put a spell on the deceased. But if you murdered someone and you didn't have a valid reason, then you would get punished. Ok, this is slightly different from the way we do things now. But the point is, there were rules.

Weddings were a non-event among the Onas. The groom would simply bring the bride home and start living together. It was common to have multiple wives, and there were rare occasions when the wives were related to each other. 

I learned all this from E. Lucas Bridges, who was born to British parents in the late 1800s in Tierra del Fuego and spent a large chunk of his adult life as a core part of the Ona tribe. He later wrote the book _Uttermost Part of the Earth_ containing observations about his life.@bridges For example, he recounts the story of an Ona man who desperately wanted a wife. When his group raided the neighbouring group for wives, he took one whose husband was killed. She was in an advanced stage of pregnancy and later gave birth to a child, who, when older became the man's second wife, and eventually became pregnant with the man's child. When this child was born, the two wives took turns to take care of it. 
